  "live_records": {
    "q-64649": {
      "dom_id": "step-q64649",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?",
      "worked_solution": "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=25\\,⟹\\,\\omega =5.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency $p$ of the external periodic force $f(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt$ coincides with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =5.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=5.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
