    "q-343755": {
      "dom_id": "step-q343755",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{7}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{7}{4}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{7}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants.",
      "worked_solution": "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $2.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =2.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =2$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[4(D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4(C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)]+4t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 4D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=\\frac{7}{4}$ and $D=0.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{7}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{7}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{7}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{7}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\n\nOnly solutions I and III match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\".",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
